# 11. How Does Our MdcFilter Become a Filter?

Consider:

```java
@Component
@Slf4j
public class MdcFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
```

When the Spring Boot application starts:

```text
SpringApplication.run()
↓
Spring ApplicationContext is created
↓
Component scanning happens
↓
Spring finds @Component
↓
Spring creates MdcFilter bean
```

Since:

```java
MdcFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter
```

the class is ultimately a Servlet Filter.

Spring Boot registers the filter into the web application's filter processing.

So, during application startup:

```text
Spring Boot starts
↓
MdcFilter bean is created
↓
MdcFilter is registered for request processing
```

Later, when a request arrives:

```text
GET /accounts/10
```

the request reaches:

```text
Tomcat
↓
Registered Filters
↓
MdcFilter
```

The filter is automatically invoked.

We do not manually call:

```java
mdcFilter.doFilter(...);
```

The web and servlet infrastructure handles that automatically.

---

# 12. Why Use OncePerRequestFilter?

A normal filter requires us to implement:

```java
doFilter(...)
```

For example:

```java
public class MyFilter implements Filter {

    @Override
    public void doFilter(...) {
        // Custom logic
    }
}
```

Spring provides a convenient base class:

```java
OncePerRequestFilter
```

Our filter becomes:

```java
public class MdcFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(...) {
        // Custom logic
    }
}
```

The main difference is:

Normal Filter
    ↓
We implement doFilter()

OncePerRequestFilter
    ↓
Spring provides doFilter()
    ↓
We implement doFilterInternal()
```

---

# 13. How Does the Request Reach doFilterInternal()?

The servlet infrastructure understands the standard Filter method:

```java
doFilter(request, response, filterChain)
```

It does not directly call:

```java
doFilterInternal()
```

The flow is:

```text
Client Request
↓
Tomcat
↓
Servlet Filter Infrastructure
↓
doFilter()
↓
OncePerRequestFilter internal logic
↓
doFilterInternal()
↓
Our custom code
```

Conceptually, `OncePerRequestFilter` works like this:

```java
public abstract class OncePerRequestFilter implements Filter {

    @Override
    public final void doFilter(
            ServletRequest request,
            ServletResponse response,
            FilterChain filterChain
    ) throws ServletException, IOException {

        // Internal Spring logic

        doFilterInternal(
                (HttpServletRequest) request,
                (HttpServletResponse) response,
                filterChain
        );
    }

    protected abstract void doFilterInternal(
            HttpServletRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response,
            FilterChain filterChain
    ) throws ServletException, IOException;
}
```

This is a simplified representation.

The actual Spring implementation contains additional logic.

The important concept is:

```text
Tomcat / Servlet Infrastructure
↓
doFilter()
↓
OncePerRequestFilter
↓
doFilterInternal()
↓
Our MdcFilter code
```

---

# 14. Our MdcFilter

Our filter looks like this:

```java
@Component
@Slf4j
public class MdcFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    private static final String REQUEST_ID = "requestId";
    private static final String REQUEST_ID_HEADER = "X-Request-Id";

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(
            HttpServletRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response,
            FilterChain filterChain
    ) throws ServletException, IOException {

        // MDC logic

    }
}
```

When the request reaches this filter, `OncePerRequestFilter` eventually invokes:

```java
doFilterInternal(...)
```

This is where our custom MDC logic starts.

For example:

```java
String requestId =
        request.getHeader(REQUEST_ID_HEADER);
```

Then we can store information in MDC:

```java
MDC.put(REQUEST_ID, requestId);
```

Then allow the request to continue:

```java
filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
```

After the request processing completes:

```java
MDC.clear();
```

---

# 15. Complete Request Flow for Our MdcFilter

Suppose the client sends:

```text
GET /accounts/10
```

The complete flow is:

```text
Client
↓
Tomcat receives request
↓
Servlet Filter Chain
↓
MdcFilter
↓
OncePerRequestFilter.doFilter()
↓
Spring performs internal filter checks
↓
MdcFilter.doFilterInternal()
↓
Read request information
↓
Add request information to MDC
↓
filterChain.doFilter()
↓
Next Filters
↓
DispatcherServlet
↓
Controller
↓
Service
↓
Repository
↓
Response processing completes
↑
Execution returns to MdcFilter
↓
MDC.clear()
↓
Response sent back to client
```

---

# 16. The Most Important Concept

A filter generally works like this:

```java
// BEFORE

chain.doFilter(request, response);

// AFTER
```

For our MDC filter:

```java
try {

    // BEFORE
    MDC.put(...);

    // Continue request
    filterChain.doFilter(request, response);

} finally {

    // AFTER
    MDC.clear();
}
```

Therefore:

```text
MDC.put()
↓
MDC information is available
↓
Controller
↓
Service
↓
Repository
↓
Request processing finishes
↓
MDC.clear()
```

The MDC values are available during the complete request processing because they are added before:

```java
filterChain.doFilter(...)
```

and removed after it finishes.

